Chapter: Asn1Write
  cites Encode chapter Asn1

 The write side of Distinguished Encoding Rules. `Asn1` is a decoder and
 nothing else, and this chapter is its mirror: it takes values we chose and
 produces the one byte string DER allows for them.

 DER permits exactly one encoding per value, so a writer has no latitude and
 every rule below is a rule the decoder enforces from the other side. Where
 the two disagree, one of them is wrong, and a round trip through `Asn1` is
 what says which.

 A length is encoded in the fewest octets that carry it. 5 is written `05`,
 never `81 05`, and a value under 128 never takes the long form at all. The
 long form carries no leading zero octet. Both rules exist so that one
 certificate cannot have two encodings, one of which was signed.

 An INTEGER is written in the fewest content octets that carry its value,
 with a leading zero octet added when the top bit of the first octet is set.
 Without that octet the value would read as negative; with it added
 unnecessarily the encoding is not minimal.

 A BIT STRING's first content octet counts the unused trailing bits. For
 every key and signature X.509 puts in one, that count is zero.

 We say:

Section: Length

  der-len : Integer -> List Integer
  der-len (n) =
    if n < 128 then [n]
    else if n < 256 then [#81, n]
    else if n < 65536 then [#82, bit-shru n 8, bit-and n 255]
    else if n < 16777216 then [#83, bit-shru n 16, bit-and (bit-shru n 8) 255, bit-and n 255]
    else [#84, bit-shru n 24, bit-and (bit-shru n 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru n 8) 255, bit-and n 255]

Section: The Tagged Value

  der-tlv : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  der-tlv (tag) (content) = [tag] & der-len (list-length content) & content

Section: Typed Writers

 The content of an INTEGER, big-endian and minimal. Zero is one octet, not
 none: an INTEGER with empty content is not a legal encoding.

  der-int-content : Integer between 0 and 2147483647 -> List Integer
  der-int-content (v) =
    if v == 0 then [0]
    else der-int-pad (der-be-min v [])

  der-be-min : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  der-be-min (v) (acc) =
    if v == 0 then acc
    else der-be-min (bit-shru v 8) ([bit-and v 255] & acc)

  der-int-pad : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-int-pad (bs) =
    if list-length bs < 1 then [0]
    else if bit-and (list-at bs 0) #80 == #80 then [0] & bs
    else bs

  der-small-int : Integer between 0 and 2147483647 -> List Integer
  der-small-int (v) = der-tlv asn1-integer (der-int-content v)

 An INTEGER whose content the caller already holds, for the values too wide
 to be an Integer: a serial number, a modulus. The content is written as
 given, so a caller handing non-minimal bytes gets an encoding our own
 decoder refuses, which is the round trip's job to catch.

  der-int-bytes : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-int-bytes (bs) = der-tlv asn1-integer bs

 TRUE is #FF and not 1. BER allows any non-zero octet; DER allows one.

  der-bool : Boolean -> List Integer
  der-bool (b) = der-tlv asn1-boolean (if b then [#FF] else [#00])

  der-null : List Integer = [asn1-null, 0]

  der-octet-string : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-octet-string (bs) = der-tlv asn1-octet-string bs

  der-bit-string : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-bit-string (bs) = der-tlv asn1-bit-string-tag ([0] & bs)

 An OBJECT IDENTIFIER takes its content bytes as given, because that is how
 `Asn1` compares one: the encoded form is canonical, so the bytes are the
 identity and decoding to numbers to re-encode them would be a way to be
 wrong for free.

  der-oid : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-oid (content) = der-tlv asn1-oid content

  der-utf8-string : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-utf8-string (bs) = der-tlv asn1-utf8-string bs

  der-printable-string : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-printable-string (bs) = der-tlv asn1-printable-string bs

  der-ia5-string : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-ia5-string (bs) = der-tlv asn1-ia5-string bs

  der-utc-time : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-utc-time (bs) = der-tlv asn1-utc-time bs

  der-general-time : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-general-time (bs) = der-tlv asn1-general-time bs

Section: Constructed

 A constructed value takes the concatenated encodings of its children as its
 content, so a caller builds the children first and joins them.

  der-sequence : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-sequence (children) = der-tlv asn1-sequence children

  der-set : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-set (children) = der-tlv asn1-set children

  der-ctx0 : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-ctx0 (children) = der-tlv asn1-ctx0 children

  der-ctx1 : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-ctx1 (children) = der-tlv asn1-ctx1 children

  der-ctx3 : List Integer -> List Integer
  der-ctx3 (children) = der-tlv asn1-ctx3 children
